label: Vertex
description: >-
  One point on a polygon ring, addressed by its position in the ring.

  ##### THE #8 RETRACTION TEST #####

  I told you a polygon was undeclarable because "a ring is genuinely
  positional -- point 4 follows point 3, and the last repeats the first to
  close the ring -- so 'order is just data' is false here; the order IS the
  datum."

  That argument is wrong, and it is wrong in exactly the way #1 was wrong. It
  proves the ELEMENT needs a positional key. It does not prove the COLLECTION
  is opaque. weir's actual rule is that a collection is addressed by key
  rather than by position -- and `seq` IS the position, promoted to a key.
  Once it is a key, the map's own ordering is irrelevant: a consumer sorts by
  `seq` and gets the ring back exactly.

  So the ban on bare arrays never applied. It bans arrays whose elements have
  no identity. A vertex has one: where it sits in the ring.
index: seq
fields:
  seq:
    type: uint16
    label: Sequence
    description: >-
      Position in the ring, from 0. The closing vertex repeats vertex 0's
      coordinates under its own final seq, so the ring closure is explicit
      data rather than an implied convention a reader has to know.
    nullable: false
    validations:
      min: 0
      max: 65535
  lng:
    # Now labellable, which it was NOT when the whole geometry was one JSON
    # string. This is the concrete payoff of the retraction.
    classification: location
    type: f64
    label: Longitude
    description: WGS84 longitude
    nullable: false
    validations:
      min: -180
      max: 180
  lat:
    classification: location
    type: f64
    label: Latitude
    description: WGS84 latitude
    nullable: false
    validations:
      min: -90
      max: 90
